Syntax v0.4 Continuation Plan
===============================================================================

Status: S0-S5 complete; S6 atomic Phase 2I activation is next.

Planning baseline: `feature/syntax` at `4560e5e6c`, rebased onto
`integration/v0.4` after the R1-R6 stack. Pull request 812 is green at this
baseline. This document changes the delivery plan only; it does not activate a
rule or change the shipping parser.

This plan supersedes the old Phase 2I-D/2I-E sequence. The refactor has one
language authority and one final source path. Recovery, typed syntax, source
semantics, certification, registry activation, document-root completion, and
the atomic product cutover have independent gates.

1. Current state
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The canonical inventory contains exactly 539 rules:

```text
canonical syntax complete  328
not yet implemented        211
                           ---
total                      539
```

The completed phases cover 328 / 539 rules. The generated Phase 2I recursive
component contains 80 of the 211 unported rules. Phase 2I-C recognizes clean
input for all 80 rules with the canonical event parser, but activates none of
them. Those rows remain `unported` with no phase assignment.

The component now has local malformed-input recovery, complete typed red-tree
views, engine-owned source semantics, and canonical certification for all 80
direct roots. The registry remains inactive until S6 performs the atomic status
transition.

The supported canonical roots are currently `Canonical + Grammar` and
`Prototype + Document`. The shipping
`mech_syntax::parser::parse(&str) -> MResult<mech_core::Program>` entry point
still uses Nom. Twenty-nine production calls across the CLI, formatter,
resolver, runtime, engine, server, bundler, and WASM controller still select
that path. S1 inventories them as removal targets.

R1-R6 changed the downstream semantic architecture. `SourceProgram`, canonical
program artifacts, Type System v1, compiler IR, memory plans, and runtime values
now have explicit owners. The new syntax path must enter those owners directly
without recreating the old parser AST pipeline.

2. Single-authority architecture
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`docs/design/specification.mec` is the sole language authority. Generated rule,
dependency, schema, port, and conformance inventories derive from it. The Nom
parser and its output do not decide accepted syntax, tree shape, whitespace,
selection, or error behavior for new work.

The authoritative parsed representation is the lossless `SyntaxSnapshot`.
Typed red-tree views are the source API for formatting, indexing, editor tools,
document inspection, and semantic compilation. Canonical rule contracts are
expressed only in terms of grammar rules, syntax kinds, tokens, typed accessors,
ranges, diagnostics, and recovery.

Execution follows this ownership chain:

```text
UTF-8 source
  -> canonical SyntaxSnapshot
  -> strict validation
  -> typed canonical document
  -> engine-owned source semantics
  -> SourceProgram / ProgramArtifact
  -> Type System v1 and memory planning
  -> runtime activation
```

The syntax crate ends at typed source and validation. It does not construct
`SourceProgram`, compiler IR, `Value`, bytecode, memory plans, or runtime cells.
The engine source frontend consumes typed syntax and constructs the semantic
program forms it owns.

Source tools use the shortest canonical route:

```text
formatter and document renderer -> typed document plus lossless tokens
source resolver and indexer      -> typed imports, exports, contexts, scopes
CLI classification               -> typed document structure
incremental/editor consumers     -> SyntaxSnapshot and diagnostics
```

There is no rule-by-rule translation to `mech_core::Program`, no compatibility
lowering tree, and no second parser authority. The final product cutover removes
the old route instead of retaining a fallback or shadow implementation.

3. Non-goals
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This continuation does not:

- change bytecode-v1, canonical encoding, dynamic ABI, native linkage, program
  artifact formats, Type System v1, or memory planner/runtime contracts;
- create compiler or runtime values inside the syntax crate;
- restore removed interpreter or REPL grammar paths;
- add a canonical-to-old-AST compatibility layer;
- use the old parser as an acceptance or tree-shape oracle;
- ship two selectable parser implementations;
- make a partial Phase 2I registry claim; or
- change the canonical language without updating the specification, generated
  inventories, conformance cases, and diagnostics together.

4. Delivery sequence
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

S0. Rebaseline and contract record
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Record the post-R1-R6 plan and update older Phase 2I documents to point to it.
Freeze the green rebased baseline and current inventory counts. This step
contains no parser behavior.

Exit gate:

- architecture, recursive-core, audit, and continuation documents agree;
- generated inventories remain unchanged; and
- the branch remains green against `integration/v0.4`.

S1. Canonical boundary and removal census
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Turn the current source surface into an enforced migration contract before
adding parser behavior.

Deliverables:

- inventory every production call through `mech_syntax::parser::parse` or its
  crate-root `mech_syntax::parse` re-export, including
  input normalization, feature gate, failure behavior, and result use;
- assign each caller its final canonical boundary: snapshot, typed document,
  source index, or engine source semantics;
- enforce the existing 80-row Phase 2I syntax schema as the rule-level output
  contract, including node, conditional-node, and transparent policies;
- define the strict-validation diagnostic schema used before typed source enters
  tools or engine semantics;
- freeze representative whole-source fixtures with expectations derived from
  the canonical specification; and
- prohibit new production calls to the old parser outside the removal census.

The current parser may keep the product green while the canonical document root
is incomplete. It supplies no expected results to canonical tests.

Exit gate:

- all 29 current production calls are inventoried at their exact source path
  and enclosing function, no unlisted call exists, and aliases cannot bypass
  the census;
- every caller has a final canonical target and cutover action;
- every Phase 2I rule has a canonical typed-syntax disposition;
- fixtures name their canonical rule/root and expected acceptance; and
- canonical parser, AST, validation, and semantic-front-end modules do not
  import or invoke Nom parser code.

S2. Recursive-core recovery
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Add local recovery for the complete 80-rule component using the ownership rules
in `recursive-executable-core.mec`.

Deliverables:

- `MISSING` insertion only at legitimate committed boundaries;
- unexpected source retained beneath `ERROR` nodes;
- delimiter-aware abandonment to the owning ancestor restart root;
- stable rule/context diagnostics and recovery actions;
- progress enforcement for every parse and recovery loop; and
- hard fuel, nesting, event, diagnostic, and recovery-byte limits.

Clean trees must remain byte-for-byte and structurally stable. Recovery must
preserve complete-form selection for tuples, parentheticals, matrices,
comprehensions, records, maps, sets, calls, slices, atoms, and tuple structures.

Exit gate:

- all 80 direct roots have malformed-input coverage;
- clean snapshots remain unchanged from Phase 2I-C;
- recovered snapshots reproduce every source byte exactly once;
- committed failures retain their diagnostic and partial parent structure; and
- adversarial shared-prefix and nesting cases stay within documented bounds.

All 80 registry rows remain inactive.

Implementation record:

- absent required syntax inserts one local `MISSING` node after commitment;
- unexpected physical source is retained below `ERROR` and abandons to the
  owning rule's delimiter or sibling boundary;
- parenthesis, bracket, brace, call, slice, table, comprehension, record, map,
  set, atom, and tuple-structure recovery preserve their selected form;
- every direct rule has a lossless malformed case tied to the syntax schema;
  and
- fuel, nesting, event, diagnostic, and cumulative recovery-byte limits have
  adversarial coverage.

S3. Typed recursive syntax
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Add zero-copy typed red-tree views under `document/ast/recursive_core/`,
mirroring frozen parser ownership.

Deliverables:

- typed casts for all unconditional and conditional Phase 2I node kinds;
- optional accessors for required children that may be absent in live syntax;
- ordered repeated-child accessors where grammar order carries meaning;
- token and delimiter access for source-preserving tools;
- explicit transparent aliases without invented wrappers; and
- schema-driven completeness generation or tests.

Exit gate:

- every node-valued schema row has a typed view;
- accessors cover clean, missing, error, and trivia-bearing forms;
- casts reject unrelated kinds; and
- typed access allocates no second tree and copies no source text.

All 80 registry rows remain inactive.

S4. Engine-owned source semantics
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Add a source frontend in the engine that consumes strictly valid typed canonical
syntax. It replaces the semantic work currently reached through
`mech_core::Program`; it does not live in the syntax crate.

Deliverables:

- define the typed document/expression inputs accepted by the engine frontend;
- give every executable Phase 2I form a semantic handling rule and every
  syntax-only form an explicit disposition;
- resolve identifiers, kinds, literals, structures, calls, patterns,
  comprehensions, FSM forms, and precedence through current compiler services;
- construct the existing engine-owned `SourceProgram` and canonical artifact
  inputs directly;
- preserve source ranges and stable diagnostic anchors into semantic errors;
- keep feature-dependent semantics behind their current compiler features; and
- reject erroneous or missing typed syntax before semantic construction.

Exit gate:

- all executable Phase 2I forms reach engine-owned source semantics;
- syntax-only forms are fully usable by their typed consumers;
- no syntax module imports engine, compiler IR, or runtime value types;
- no engine source-semantic module accepts `mech_core::Program`; and
- Type System v1, artifact, memory, and runtime canaries remain unchanged.

All 80 registry rows remain inactive.

S5. Canonical certification
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Certify the component from the specification and canonical schema before
changing inventory status.

Deliverables:

- direct accepted and rejected cases for every Phase 2I rule;
- exact lossless tree and typed-accessor snapshots;
- ambiguity cases for every shared prefix and complete-form selection rule;
- generated/property cases for nesting, repeated operands, trivia, Unicode,
  delimiters, and boundary whitespace;
- recovery and resource-limit cases tied to stable diagnostic fields;
- semantic cases derived from the specification for executable forms; and
- downstream canaries for the canonical consumer of each typed family.

`MATCH-001` remains part of the canonical grammar and conformance corpus. It is
not treated as a difference from another parser.

Exit gate:

- the 80-rule matrix passes under full and base syntax profiles;
- ambiguity, recovery, and resource suites pass;
- semantic fixtures produce the specified canonical program behavior; and
- no test imports the old parser to establish an expected result.

All 80 registry rows remain inactive until this gate is complete.

S6. Atomic Phase 2I activation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Update `ports.tsv` for all 80 rules in one change and regenerate the canonical
port registry. Each member becomes Phase 2I with certified syntax, typed views,
recovery, and source-semantic disposition.

Replace the registry's pre-plan `lowering-status` field with an explicit
`semantic-status` field before assigning Phase 2I. Replace the ambiguous
`parity-verified` syntax label with `certified`. Existing closed phases are
reclassified from their canonical evidence; the retiring lowerers are not a
status source.

Expected inventory after activation:

```text
canonical syntax certified 408
not yet implemented        131
                           ---
total                      539
```

Exit gate:

- the generated SCC report still identifies exactly the reviewed 80 members;
- every row has parse, recovery, typed-view, semantic, ambiguity, and
  resource-limit evidence;
- generated registries and inventory tests pass; and
- the public root matrix is unchanged.

S7. Complete the canonical document root
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Regenerate dependency and SCC reports after Phase 2I activation. The 131 rows
that remain unported are an inventory count, not an assumption that every row
belongs to the shipping document closure. Partition them into generated
document dependencies, maintained roots, and historical or non-syntax entries.
Give every row an explicit current disposition. Port the document closure in
generated order, one complete SCC or closed acyclic batch at a time.

Every batch repeats the canonical gates:

```text
schema -> clean parse -> recovery -> typed views -> source semantics
       -> canonical certification -> atomic batch activation
```

The objective is a complete canonical `Document` root for sibling Mech and
Mechdown constructs. It must preserve all source bytes and expose title,
section, annotation, fenced-code, import/export/context, comment, and range
structure to its typed consumers.

Exit gate:

- `Canonical + Document` is the supported production-capable root;
- every rule needed by it is registered and certified;
- every remaining inventory row has a reviewed current disposition;
- tools and engine source semantics cover the complete canonical fixture corpus;
- malformed documents produce total snapshots and structured diagnostics; and
- no product caller has switched early.

S8. Atomic product cutover and removal
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Replace the complete S1 caller inventory by consumer class:

- formatter, HTML, CLI classification, source indexing, and document tooling
  consume canonical snapshots and typed documents;
- resolver and incremental consumers retain canonical source revisions and
  typed indexes;
- engine, runtime, config, and WASM execution enter through engine-owned source
  semantics; and
- bundles publish canonical program artifacts rather than serialized old parser
  trees.

The same milestone removes:

- the Nom parser and direct-rule infrastructure;
- the `document/lower/legacy` tree;
- the old source-to-`mech_core::Program` route and internal callers;
- obsolete parser feature coupling and duplicate formatter/index paths; and
- tests whose only purpose was to compare or preserve the removed parser.

There is no runtime selector, shadow mode, compatibility adapter, or fallback.
Any public API break is explicit and documented in the cutover change.

Exit gate:

- the removal census contains zero remaining production calls;
- repository scans find no Nom parser path or compatibility lowerer;
- all source tools use canonical typed syntax;
- all execution consumers use engine source semantics;
- static native, WASM source, no-std, warning, artifact, and runtime jobs pass;
  and
- full integration CI is green with one language authority and one source path.

5. Validation policy
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each milestone runs the checks that prove its local gate:

- generated rule, port, dependency, schema, and SCC inventories;
- full and base syntax test profiles;
- grammar conformance under explicit feature sets;
- Mika-only and reduced/no-std boundary checks;
- formatting, static architecture, module layout, and warning policy; and
- focused recovery, typed-view, semantic, ambiguity, or complexity suites.

S6 and S8 require the full integration workflow because they change an
authoritative registry or the shipping source path. S8 also requires static
native and WASM source distributions plus the end-to-end compiler/runtime
canaries used by `integration/v0.4`.

Tests compare canonical structure, values, ranges, diagnostic codes, and
recovery actions. English diagnostic wording is not an oracle.

6. Review and branch shape
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Use one reviewable branch per numbered milestone. Recovery, typed syntax, and
engine source semantics stay separate even if later work begins in a stack. A
branch targets the last accepted syntax milestone and is rebased when its base
moves.

Generated files and their source inventories change together. Phase 2I
activation is isolated so reviewers can verify the exact 80-row transition. The
product cutover is one coordinated removal milestone; implementation branches
may prepare canonical modules beforehand, but merged production code never
exposes two selectable source paths.

7. Completion definition
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The syntax continuation is complete when the canonical document parser is the
only production source parser, all 539 canonical rules have a current reviewed
disposition, tools consume canonical typed syntax, execution consumes the
engine-owned source-semantic path, and Nom plus compatibility lowering are
absent from production dependencies.
